<!doctype html>
{%- comment -%}
  no-js is set up front and swapped for js by the inline script below, so the
  CSS can provide fallbacks for anything JS would otherwise toggle open.
{%- endcomment -%}
<html class="no-js" lang="{{ request.locale.iso_code }}">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <meta name="theme-color" content="{{ settings.brand_colour | default: '#df3c22' }}">
    <link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical_url }}">

    {%- comment -%}
      Images and the theme bundle come from the shop's own origin, which is
      already connected by the time the HTML lands — nothing to warm there.

      `cdn.shopify.com` is a DIFFERENT origin, and the installed apps
      (Judge.me, Inbox) put a RENDER-BLOCKING stylesheet on it. That meant a
      cold DNS + TCP + TLS handshake — measured at ~195ms on this store — before
      the file could even start downloading, and rendering waited on all of it.
      Warming the connection in the first bytes of <head> lets that handshake
      overlap the HTML parse instead of following it.

      preconnect (not dns-prefetch): the DNS lookup is the cheap third of the
      cost; the TLS negotiation is the expensive part worth doing early.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://cdn.shopify.com" crossorigin>

    {%- comment -%}
      INTRO SPLASH — the decision has to happen before the first paint.

      This inline script marks the document when the splash has already been
      seen this session, so CSS can keep it display:none from the very first
      frame. Doing it in JS after load would flash the overlay on every page
      view, which is exactly the thing that makes splash screens feel cheap.

      It is deliberately tiny and synchronous: it must run before the body
      paints, and it touches nothing but one class.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if settings.splash_enabled -%}
      <script>
        (function () {
          try {
            if (sessionStorage.getItem('mts.splash') === '1') {
              document.documentElement.classList.add('mts-splash-done');
            } else {
              sessionStorage.setItem('mts.splash', '1');
            }
          } catch (e) {
            /* private mode: show it once, never trap anyone */
            document.documentElement.classList.add('mts-splash-done');
          }
        })();
      </script>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      The icon is the store's real one, lifted byte-for-byte from
      mytapestore.com.au (wp-content/uploads/2023/08/fav.png) — the black
      tape-roll glyph, not the red ring the redesign shipped.

      It is 30x30 and that is the ONLY size that exists: every WordPress variant
      (-32x32, -180x180, -192x192, -270x270, -scaled) 302s, and the site logo is
      244x43, so there is no larger copy of the mark to recover. The 32px link
      therefore serves the original file untouched; the touch icon is scaled up
      from it and is necessarily soft.

      A merchant-uploaded favicon in theme settings still wins if one is set.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if settings.favicon != blank -%}
      <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="{{ settings.favicon | image_url: width: 32, height: 32 }}">
      <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="{{ settings.favicon | image_url: width: 180, height: 180 }}">
    {%- else -%}
      <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="32x32" href="{{ 'mts-favicon-32.png' | asset_url }}">
      <link rel="icon" type="image/png" sizes="180x180" href="{{ 'mts-favicon.png' | asset_url }}">
      <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="{{ 'mts-favicon.png' | asset_url }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      The collection banner is the largest paint on a category page, so it is
      preloaded from the head rather than waiting to be found in the body. Same
      srcset as the <img>, so the browser preloads the exact file it will use —
      a mismatched preload downloads the image twice.

      THIS MUST MIRROR mts-collection.liquid'S BANNER PRECEDENCE EXACTLY.
      Preloading a different file from the one the <img> renders is worse than
      not preloading at all: the browser downloads both, and the one that
      actually paints is the one that WASN'T given priority. When the banner
      moved to the `custom.banner` metafield this block still pointed at
      collection.image, so every category page fetched the old banner at high
      priority and the real one late — the "images load late" symptom.

      The section-level `banner_image` override is still not visible from the
      layout; it is unset on this store, and if it is ever set the fallback here
      is merely a redundant fetch, not a wrong paint.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- liquid
      assign pre_banner = blank
      if template.name == 'collection'
        assign pre_mf = collection.metafields.custom.banner.value
        if pre_mf != blank
          assign pre_banner = pre_mf
        elsif collection.image
          assign pre_banner = collection.image
        endif
      endif
    -%}
    {%- if pre_banner != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The 24px placeholder is a CSS background-image on `.colban`, and the
        preload scanner CANNOT see CSS backgrounds — it is only discovered once
        styles are computed, which is well after the real banner has already
        been requested. So the thing meant to paint FIRST was reliably arriving
        LAST, and the band sat empty in the meantime.

        Preloading it puts it in the very first round trip. It costs a few
        hundred bytes and is same-origin, so it cannot meaningfully delay the
        full-size banner below it.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <link rel="preload" as="image" fetchpriority="high"
            href="{{ pre_banner | image_url: width: 24 }}">
      <link rel="preload" as="image" fetchpriority="high"
            href="{{ pre_banner | image_url: width: 1600 }}"
            imagesrcset="{{ pre_banner | image_url: width: 800 }} 800w,
                         {{ pre_banner | image_url: width: 1200 }} 1200w,
                         {{ pre_banner | image_url: width: 1600 }} 1600w,
                         {{ pre_banner | image_url: width: 2000 }} 2000w"
            imagesizes="100vw">
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      Same treatment for the product page's LCP, the gallery's main image. It
      sits behind the breadcrumbs and a gallery wrapper, so the preload scanner
      reaches it noticeably later than it reaches a banner.

      MUST MATCH the srcset/sizes in sections/mts-product.liquid exactly —
      widths, order and `sizes` alike — or the browser preloads one candidate
      and paints another, which costs a whole extra download.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if template.name == 'product' and product.featured_media -%}
      <link rel="preload" as="image" fetchpriority="high"
            href="{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 800 }}"
            imagesrcset="{{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 400 }} 400w,
                         {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 600 }} 600w,
                         {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 800 }} 800w,
                         {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 1000 }} 1000w,
                         {{ product.featured_media | image_url: width: 1360 }} 1360w"
            imagesizes="(min-width: 960px) 680px, 100vw">
    {%- endif -%}

    <title>
      {{ page_title }}
      {%- if current_tags %} &ndash; {{ 'general.meta.tags' | t: tags: current_tags | join: ', ' }}{% endif -%}
      {%- if current_page != 1 %} &ndash; {{ 'general.meta.page' | t: page: current_page }}{% endif -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        The " &ndash; My Tape Store" suffix used to be appended here. Removed on
        request: every title already carries the product or category name, the
        store name added ~17 characters to every one of them, and Google
        truncates around 60 — so the suffix was pushing real words out of the
        result and adding nothing a shopper searching for tape needs to see.
      {%- endcomment -%}
    </title>
    {%- comment -%}
      The HOME PAGE had no meta description at all. Shopify's homepage
      description lives in Online Store > Preferences, which is not exposed in
      the Admin API, so it cannot be set the way the 132 product and 40
      collection descriptions were. A theme-level fallback covers it and stays
      editable in theme settings.

      Order matters: a real page_description always wins, so setting the
      preference later silently takes over from this without a code change.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if page_description -%}
      <meta name="description" content="{{ page_description | escape }}">
    {%- elsif template.name == 'index' and settings.home_meta_description != blank -%}
      <meta name="description" content="{{ settings.home_meta_description | escape }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    {% render 'mts-meta-tags' %}

    {%- comment -%}
      Preload the two faces that paint above the fold — same pair the React app
      preloads, so first paint uses the real type rather than a fallback.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {{ 'Inter-400.woff2' | asset_url | preload_tag: as: 'font', type: 'font/woff2', crossorigin: 'anonymous' }}
    {{ 'Sora-var.woff2' | asset_url | preload_tag: as: 'font', type: 'font/woff2', crossorigin: 'anonymous' }}

    {%- comment -%}
      One bundle, built from the React stylesheets by mytapestore-shopify/build-css.sh.
      It is the ONLY stylesheet this theme loads — no vendor CSS runs alongside it,
      which is what lets every rem, colour and size stay byte-identical to the redesign.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The file on disk is mts-styles.css.liquid, but Shopify STRIPS the .liquid
      extension when resolving asset_url — so it must be requested as
      'mts-styles.css'. Asking for the full filename returns a 404 and the store
      renders with no CSS at all.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {{ 'mts-styles.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

    <script>document.documentElement.className = document.documentElement.className.replace('no-js', 'js');</script>
    <script src="{{ 'mts-theme.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>

    {%- comment -%}
      Judge.me core. This theme has none of the app's default anchors, so the
      settings script is output directly (Judge.me's documented manual install)
      rather than relying on the app embed being toggled on. The widgets
      themselves are placed in mts-product.liquid.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if shop.metafields.judgeme.settings -%}
      {{ shop.metafields.judgeme.settings }}
      {{ shop.metafields.judgeme.html_miracle_0 }}
    {%- endif -%}

    {{ content_for_header }}
  </head>

  <body class="template-{{ template.name | handle }}{% if template.suffix %} template-suffix-{{ template.suffix | handle }}{% endif %}">
{%- if settings.splash_enabled -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        No JS is required to REMOVE this. The fade-out and the final
        `visibility:hidden; pointer-events:none` are both keyframe steps, so if
        scripting fails, is blocked, or throws, the overlay still clears itself
        and the shopper is never sealed behind it. JS only tidies the node away
        afterwards. aria-hidden + inert keeps it out of the accessibility tree
        so a screen reader never announces it at all.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <div class="mts-splash" data-mts-splash aria-hidden="true">
        <div class="mts-splash__glow"></div>
        {%- liquid
          assign splash_logo = 'mts-logo-light.png' | asset_url
          if settings.logo_light != blank
            assign splash_logo = settings.logo_light | image_url: width: 720
          endif
        -%}
        <div class="mts-splash__mark">
          {%- comment -%}
            The light wordmark is used directly rather than inverting the dark
            one with a filter — inverting flattens the two-tone mark to a single
            white silhouette and throws away the red.

            The shine is a separate layer MASKED to the same artwork, so the
            highlight travels across the letterforms themselves instead of
            sweeping over a rectangle. That is the difference between a glint
            and a reflection sliding over a box.
          {%- endcomment -%}
          <span class="mts-splash__logo" style="--mts-splash-logo:url({{ splash_logo }})">
            <img src="{{ splash_logo }}" alt="" width="300" height="52" fetchpriority="high">
            <span class="mts-splash__shine" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </span>
          <span class="mts-splash__rule"></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    {%- endif -%}
    <a class="skip" href="#main">{{ 'general.accessibility.skip_to_content' | t }}</a>

    {% sections 'header-group' %}

    {{ content_for_layout }}

    {% sections 'footer-group' %}

    {% render 'mts-call-button' %}
    {% render 'mts-chat' %}
    {% render 'mts-newsletter-popup' %}

    {%- comment -%}
      Cart state the JS needs on every page (header count, free-shipping bar,
      quick-add). Rendered rather than fetched so the first paint is correct.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <script type="application/json" id="mts-cart-bootstrap">
      {"item_count": {{ cart.item_count | json }}, "total_price": {{ cart.total_price | json }}, "currency": {{ cart.currency.iso_code | json }}}
    </script>
    <script>
      window.MTS = window.MTS || {};
      window.MTS.routes = {
        root: {{ routes.root_url | json }},
        cart: {{ routes.cart_url | json }},
        cartAdd: {{ routes.cart_add_url | json }},
        cartChange: {{ routes.cart_change_url | json }},
        cartUpdate: {{ routes.cart_update_url | json }},
        search: {{ routes.search_url | json }},
        predictiveSearch: {{ routes.predictive_search_url | json }}
      };
      window.MTS.moneyFormat = {{ shop.money_format | json }};
      window.MTS.freeShipThreshold = {{ settings.free_shipping_threshold | default: 100 | times: 100 | json }};
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
